From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob <rob@performanceinsights.ai>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:48:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] apparmor: fix label refcount leak in apparmor_unix_stream_connect()

Since commit 88fec3526e84 ("apparmor: make sure unix socket labeling
is correctly updated."), apparmor_sk_alloc_security() takes a
reference on the current task's label for every new sock. That
includes newsk, which unix_stream_connect() allocates through
unix_create1() before it calls security_unix_stream_connect().

apparmor_unix_stream_connect() then assigns new_ctx->label a reference
to the peer's label without putting the one taken in sk_alloc, so every
successful AF_UNIX stream connect leaks one reference to the connecting
task's label. Commit f3c0675bb9e0 removed the AA_BUG() that asserted
new_ctx->label was still NULL here, but did not drop the reference.

The leaked references keep the label, its profiles and their policy
namespace allocated forever once the profile is removed or replaced.
On a host that loads and removes a profile and namespace per container
this shows up as steadily growing unreclaimable slab.

Put the old label before the assignment, as apparmor_socket_post_create(),
apparmor_socket_socketpair() and apparmor_sk_clone_security() already do.

Fixes: 88fec3526e84 ("apparmor: make sure unix socket labeling is correctly updated.")
---
 security/apparmor/lsm.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/security/apparmor/lsm.c b/security/apparmor/lsm.c
--- a/security/apparmor/lsm.c
+++ b/security/apparmor/lsm.c
@@ -1206,6 +1206,7 @@ static int apparmor_unix_stream_connect(struct sock *sk, struct sock *peer_sk,
 	/* newsk doesn't go through post_create, but does go through
 	 * security_sk_alloc()
 	 */
+	aa_put_label(rcu_dereference_protected(new_ctx->label, true));
 	rcu_assign_pointer(new_ctx->label,
 			   aa_get_label(rcu_dereference_protected(peer_ctx->label,
 								  true)));
